  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The National Aeronautics and Space Administration obligated $354M in federal contracts in FY2027 to date. The agency recorded 408 awards with an average award size of $867K. Obligations fell 97.8% year over year. Fiscal-year comparisons show FY22 obligations of $15.2B, FY23 obligations of $16.1B, FY24 obligations of $15B, FY25 obligations of $15.8B, and FY26 obligations of $16B. Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology) is the largest NAICS category at $199M, followed by Nonscheduled Chartered Freight Air Transportation at $91.4M. THE JOHNS HOPKINS UNIVERSITY APPLIED PHYSICS LABORATORY LLC is the top vendor at $85.3M, followed by CALIFORNIA INSTITUTE OF TECHNOLOGY at $78.4M and BLUE ORIGIN, LLC at $46.1M. All figures are contract obligations and current-year totals are partial (fiscal year to date).",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has NASA's contract spending changed year over year?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 97.8% year over year. The agency obligated $354M in FY27 to date after obligations of $16B in FY26 and $15.8B in FY25. Earlier annual obligations were $15B in FY24, $16.1B in FY23, and $15.2B in FY22. The FY27 total is a partial fiscal-year figure."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What are NASA's largest contract categories and who wins the money?",
        "body": "Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology) is the largest category at $199M, followed by Nonscheduled Chartered Freight Air Transportation at $91.4M and Facilities Support Services at $15.5M. THE JOHNS HOPKINS UNIVERSITY APPLIED PHYSICS LABORATORY LLC received $85.3M, CALIFORNIA INSTITUTE OF TECHNOLOGY received $78.4M, and BLUE ORIGIN, LLC received $46.1M. The top ten vendors include IMPULSE SPACE, INC. at $37.1M and AMENTUM SPACEPORT LLC at $12.4M."
      }
    ]
  },
